    "q-113105": {
      "dom_id": "step-q113105",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$",
      "worked_solution": "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
