    "q-36366": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36366",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
