    "q-81852": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81852",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & b \\\\ 4 & c & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-81852-a",
